Run the logger on the main thread instead of a dedicated QThread.

On this host every guest pthread is a Web Worker, and Chromium compiles
the whole 82 MB quickshell module again for each worker (hundreds of MB of
machine code per thread — see
docs/browser-support.md#quickshell-qml-limits). The logger thread exists
upstream to keep log I/O off the UI thread; here it buys no parallelism
(one-CPU kernel) and costs a full compiled copy of the module.

Since 0.3.2, LogManager::init() creates ThreadLogging on the main thread
and LogManager::initThreadLogging() later moves it to a new QThread. That
move is dropped here, so the logger stays where it was created, and
initFs() calls ThreadLogging::initFs() directly: a
BlockingQueuedConnection to an object on the calling thread deadlocks.
After initFs(), log-file writes queue on the main loop, so disk logs
from the initial QML load (before exec()) are delayed and can be lost
if the program crashes during that load; console output is unaffected.
The 0.3.1 form of this patch behaved the same way.

diff --git a/src/core/logging.cpp b/src/core/logging.cpp
index 1dc4a56..2a5c266 100644
--- a/src/core/logging.cpp
+++ b/src/core/logging.cpp
@@ -342,29 +342,14 @@ void LogManager::init(
 }
 
 void LogManager::initThreadLogging() {
-	auto* instance = LogManager::instance();
-
-	qCDebug(logLogging) << "Moving logger to dedicated thread...";
-
-	auto* thread = new QThread();
-	instance->threadLogging->setParent(nullptr);
-	instance->threadLogging->moveToThread(thread);
-	thread->start();
-
-	qCDebug(logLogging) << "Logger thread initialized.";
+	qCDebug(logLogging) << "Keeping logger on the main thread.";
 }
 
 void initLogCategoryLevel(const char* name, QtMsgType defaultLevel) {
 	LogManager::instance()->defaultLevels.insert(QLatin1StringView(name), defaultLevel);
 }
 
-void LogManager::initFs() {
-	QMetaObject::invokeMethod(
-	    LogManager::instance()->threadLogging,
-	    &ThreadLogging::initFs,
-	    Qt::BlockingQueuedConnection
-	);
-}
+void LogManager::initFs() { LogManager::instance()->threadLogging->initFs(); }
 
 QString LogManager::rulesString() const { return this->mRulesString; }
 QtMsgType LogManager::defaultLevel() const { return this->mDefaultLevel; }
